Z||000001||BBBAD GT||04-05-2001||08:56 PM||||Awwww man....it leaks?  It 'was' a rebuilt full size blazer unit, a Carbone I think, bought when the GA brakes went on.<P>Just for reference, I did drive around a while with the stock MC, and I really had to push down on the pedel for any action to happen.  So I'd say go bigger than stock.<P>So much for rebuilt units....<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">       <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jaei Sun||04-06-2001||12:39 AM||||Jeff, I should have specified... it's the clutch master cylinder.  As far as I can see the brake MC is fine... no problems there.<P>Ya mentioned that you bled the clutch line in mid summer or so when you got it.  Well a month ago I checked the fluid level and it was sightly below the "add line".  So that's where it's going I guess.  Maybe that's where the air is getting into the line causing the clutch problem.<P>Either way I want to replace the Clutch MC instead of rebuild it, flush the line, and refill it with this brake fluid that Chrysler has that doesn't boil.  I've noticed that the slave is right next to the exhaust piping and my Dad said that brake fluid may be boiling and causing more air to get in the line.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||10
